ace.rs-76-         .max_depth(1)\ncrates/skillet-cli/src/workspace.rs-86-             None => continue,\ncrates/skillet-cli/src/workspace.rs-87-         };\ncrates/skillet-cli/src/workspace.rs:88:         let name = match file_name.strip_suffix(\".fragment.pan\") {\ncrates/skillet-cli/src/workspace.rs-89-             Some(n) => n.to_string(),\ncrates/skillet-cli/src/workspace.rs-90-             None => continue,\ncrates/skillet-cli/src/workspace.rs-91-         };\ncrates/skillet-cli/src/workspace.rs-92-         let content = std::fs::read_to_string(path)\ncrates/skillet-cli/src/workspace.rs:93:             .with_context(|| format!(\"failed to read fragment '{}'\", path.display()))?;\ncrates/skillet-cli/src/workspace.rs-94-         map.insert(name, content);\ncrates/skillet-cli/src/workspace.rs-95-     }\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/manifest.json-1- {\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/manifest.json-2-   \"goalRunId\": \"goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3\",\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/manifest.json:3:   \"goal\": \"Move the lint pipeline's Phase 2 ref extraction (in `crates/skillet/src/lint/pipeline.rs`) and the `unused_fragment` rule (in `crates/skillet/src/lint/rules/unused_fragment.rs`) to use the new parser (`crate::compiler::parse::PanParse` and `crate::compiler::PanSource`) instead of regexes.\\n\\nSpecifically:\\n1. In `ParsedRefs::extract` (in `crates/skillet/src/refs.rs`), replace the regex-based extraction of typed refs and untyped backticks with the new parser. Parse the source using `Pa... [truncated]\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/manifest.json-4-   \"launchedAt\": \"2026-05-27T07:51:04.532Z\",\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/manifest.json-5-   \"lifecycleStatus\": \"active\",\ndocs/diy.md-84- \ndocs/diy.md-85- - What *are* the tokens of `.pan`? Markdown is structural — what counts as a token vs. what counts as opaque body text?\ndocs/diy.md:86: - How do you handle the special constructs (`{{> name }}`, `var::FOO`, `ref::path`, `cmd::name`, `skill::name`)? Are they whole tokens, or are they multi-token sequences (e.g., `{{>` + name + `}}`)?\ndocs/diy.md-87- - Frontmatter — is it a token, or is it parsed separately by a YAML/TOML library before the lexer even runs?\ndocs/diy.md-88- - Code fences — when the lexer sees ```` ``` ```` what state does it enter? How does it know when to exit?\ndocs/diy.md-97- - The naive \"is this character alphanumeric?\" check via `char::is_alphanumeric` does a UTF-8 lookup. For ASCII-only sub-grammars (identifiers, keywords), a 256-byte lookup table is faster *and* makes the code intent clearer.\ndocs/diy.md-98- - Don't allocate per token. Tokens carry `&str` slices into the source or `Range<u32>` byte offsets. No `String`.\ndocs/diy.md:99: - Be careful with `{{` and `{{>` — your lexer needs lookahead. Decide how much (one byte? two?).\ndocs/diy.md-100- \ndocs/diy.md-101- **Acceptance:**\ndocs/diy.md-122- **Design questions:**\ndocs/diy.md-123- \ndocs/diy.md:124: - Should fragments (`{{> name }}`) be parsed as a single `Fragment` block, or as a `Markdown` block containing an interpolated expression? The answer drives whether substitution happens during parse or during compile.\ndocs/diy.md-125- - Frontmatter — own AST type or just `serde_yaml::Value`? What's the tradeoff?\ndocs/diy.md-126- - Code fences — opaque body string, or do you also tokenize what's inside? (Hint: the body is opaque to skillet; treat it as a single string.)\ndocs/diy.md-150- \ndocs/diy.md-151- - What's the right return type? A struct with `output`, `diagnostics`, `tokens`, `refs_used`? Or do you accept an output `String` by `&mut`? Both work; what are the testability implications?\ndocs/diy.md:152: - How is the `CompileContext` (resolved fragments, vars, the skill registry) constructed? When does it get built — once per project, once per file?\ndocs/diy.md-153- - Where does fragment expansion happen — inline as you walk, or as a transformation pass that produces an \"expanded AST\"? Pros and cons of each.\ndocs/diy.md-154- \ndocs/diy.md-151- - What's the right return type? A struct with `output`, `diagnostics`, `tokens`, `refs_used`? Or do you accept an output `String` by `&mut`? Both work; what are the testability implications?\ndocs/diy.md-152- - How is the `CompileContext` (resolved fragments, vars, the skill registry) constructed? When does it get built — once per project, once per file?\ndocs/diy.md:153: - Where does fragment expansion happen — inline as you walk, or as a transformation pass that produces an \"expanded AST\"? Pros and cons of each.\ndocs/diy.md-154- \ndocs/diy.md-155- **Design questions:**\ndocs/diy.md-160- **Gotchas:**\ndocs/diy.md-161- \ndocs/diy.md:162: - Fragment expansion needs a guard. Not graph cycle detection — just a `HashSet<&str>` of \"fragments currently being expanded in this chain\" passed down the recursion. Five lines. Forgetting this turns `{{> a }} → {{> b }} → {{> a }}` into a stack overflow.\ndocs/diy.md-163- - Don't reach for `Box<dyn Rule>`. Plain `fn validate_ref(...)` functions called from the `match`. You have under ten rules total.\ndocs/diy.md-164- - Resist deferring work to a \"second pass\" until you have a rule that actually requires it. Cross-file checks (does this `skill::foo` exist?) are one obvious case — they need every file parsed first. That's a function called after the per-file loop, not a `Deferred` queue.\ndocs/diy.md-168- - Running `skillet build` on an existing skill produces a `SKILL.md` byte-for-byte identical to whatever skillet produces today.\ndocs/diy.md-169- - Introducing an unknown `ref::` produces a diagnostic with the correct line and column.\ndocs/diy.md:170: - Introducing a fragment cycle produces a diagnostic, not a stack overflow.\ndocs/diy.md-171- \ndocs/diy.md-172- **Reading:**\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/steps/orchestrator/sessions/init-r1.session-2- {\"type\":\"model_change\",\"id\":\"288d11be\",\"parentId\":null,\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-27T07:51:07.986Z\",\"provider\":\"github-copilot\",\"modelId\":\"gpt-5.4\"}\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/steps/orchestrator/sessions/init-r1.session-3- {\"type\":\"thinking_level_change\",\"id\":\"0c493762\",\"parentId\":\"288d11be\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-27T07:51:07.986Z\",\"thinkingLevel\":\"medium\"}\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/steps/orchestrator/sessions/init-r1.session:4: {\"type\":\"message\",\"id\":\"f468c32f\",\"parentId\":\"0c493762\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-27T07:51:07.997Z\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Goal Step List:\\nMove the lint pipeline's Phase 2 ref extraction (in `crates/skillet/src/lint/pipeline.rs`) and the `unused_fragment` rule (in `crates/skillet/src/lint/rules/unused_fragment.rs`) to use the new parser (`crate::compiler::parse::PanParse` and `crate::compiler::PanSource`) instead of regexes.\\n\\nSpecifically:\\n1. In `ParsedRefs::ex... [truncated]\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/steps/orchestrator/sessions/init-r1.session-5- {\"type\":\"message\",\"id\":\"7a76580f\",\"parentId\":\"f468c32f\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-27T07:51:30.636Z\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"**Evaluating functional acceptance**\\n\\nI think functional acceptance shouldn’t get too detailed about mappings. It seems typed references of supported kinds are recognized like before, and markdown links are still acknowledged, but unsupported parser reference kinds should be ignored. Then, there's another step for the fragment ru... [truncated]\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/steps/orchestrator/sessions/init-r1.session-6- {\"type\":\"message\",\"id\":\"89e7806c\",\"parentId\":\"7a76580f\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-27T07:51:30.659Z\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"toolResult\",\"toolCallId\":\"call_r0zGEkbP8MbiyBR1b9FN2qS5|... [truncated]\n\n[200 matches limit reached. Use limit=400 for more, or refine pattern. 50.0KB limit reached. Some lines truncated to 500 chars. Use read tool to see full lines]"}],"details":{"matchLimitReached":200,"truncation":{"content":".pi/skills/professor/SKILL.md-63- - Error tokens vs aborting the stream on invalid input\n.pi/skills/professor/SKILL.md-64- - Why ASCII-only identifiers warrant a lookup table over `char::is_alphanumeric`\n.pi/skills/professor/SKILL.md:65: - Lookahead requirements (`{{` vs `{{>`)\n.pi/skills/professor/SKILL.md-66- - Zero-allocation token design (`&str` slices or `Range<u32>`, not `String`)\n.pi/skills/professor/SKILL.md-67- \nCONTEXT.md-4- \nCONTEXT.md-5- - **Skill**: A folder containing a `SKILL.md` file (compiled output) and optionally scripts, references, and assets. Follows the [Agent Skills](https://agentskills.io/home) open format.\nCONTEXT.md:6: - **Skill source**: A `{name}.pan` file — the authoring format that compiles to `SKILL.md`. Contains template directives (fragment includes, typed refs, vars).\nCONTEXT.md-7- - **Fragment**: A reusable block of skill instructions. File named `{name}.fragment.pan`, lives in the workspace-global fragments directory. Included via `{{> name }}` syntax. Block-level only, no parameters.\nCONTEXT.md-8- - **Ref**: A reference from a skill to an external entity. First-class concept with types: `ref::` (file path), `cmd::` (CLI command), `skill::` (another skill in workspace), `var::` (workspace variable), `env::` (declared environment variable).\nCONTEXT.md-5- - **Skill**: A folder containing a `SKILL.md` file (compiled output) and optionally scripts, references, and assets. Follows the [Agent Skills](https://agentskills.io/home) open format.\nCONTEXT.md-6- - **Skill source**: A `{name}.pan` file — the authoring format that compiles to `SKILL.md`. Contains template directives (fragment includes, typed refs, vars).\nCONTEXT.md:7: - **Fragment**: A reusable block of skill instructions. File named `{name}.fragment.pan`, lives in the workspace-global fragments directory. Included via `{{> name }}` syntax. Block-level only, no parameters.\nCONTEXT.md-8- - **Ref**: A reference from a skill to an external entity. First-class concept with types: `ref::` (file path), `cmd::` (CLI command), `skill::` (another skill in workspace), `var::` (workspace variable), `env::` (declared environment variable).\nCONTEXT.md-9- - **Workspace**: A directory containing a `skillet.toml` and a skills directory. The unit of analysis for cross-skill operations (duplication detection, budget totals).\nCONTEXT.md-12- - **Activation cost**: Tokens consumed when a skill is triggered — the full compiled SKILL.md.\nCONTEXT.md-13- - **Transitive cost**: Activation cost plus all files the skill instructs the agent to read.\nCONTEXT.md:14: - **Lockfile**: `skillet.lock` — committed workspace-level file recording source hashes, compiled hashes, token counts, fragment usage, and ref inventories per skill. Enables fast staleness checks and PR-diffable impact tracking.\nCONTEXT.md-15- - **SourceUnit**: A named piece of pre-read source content passed to the lib for compilation or analysis. Carries `name` and `content` (the raw `.pan` text) but no filesystem paths. The generic input type for skills, agent files, and any future compilable source type.\nCONTEXT.md-16- - **CompileContext**: The full bundle of data the lib needs to compile a single source unit: a `SourceUnit`, resolved fragment contents, and build config. Assembled by the CLI before calling into lib.\nCONTEXT.md-14- - **Lockfile**: `skillet.lock` — committed workspace-level file recording source hashes, compiled hashes, token counts, fragment usage, and ref inventories per skill. Enables fast staleness checks and PR-diffable impact tracking.\nCONTEXT.md-15- - **SourceUnit**: A named piece of pre-read source content passed to the lib for compilation or analysis. Carries `name` and `content` (the raw `.pan` text) but no filesystem paths. The generic input type for skills, agent files, and any future compilable source type.\nCONTEXT.md:16: - **CompileContext**: The full bundle of data the lib needs to compile a single source unit: a `SourceUnit`, resolved fragment contents, and build config. Assembled by the CLI before calling into lib.\nCONTEXT.md-17- \n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/goal-execution-plan.json-1- {\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/goal-execution-plan.json-2-   \"goalRunId\": \"goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3\",\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/goal-execution-plan.json:3:   \"goal\": \"Move the lint pipeline's Phase 2 ref extraction (in `crates/skillet/src/lint/pipeline.rs`) and the `unused_fragment` rule (in `crates/skillet/src/lint/rules/unused_fragment.rs`) to use the new parser (`crate::compiler::parse::PanParse` and `crate::compiler::PanSource`) instead of regexes.\\n\\nSpecifically:\\n1. In `ParsedRefs::extract` (in `crates/skillet/src/refs.rs`), replace the regex-based extraction of typed refs and untyped backticks with the new parser. Parse the source using `Pa... [truncated]\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/goal-execution-plan.json-4-   \"steps\": [\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/goal-execution-plan.json-5-     {\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/goal-execution-plan.json-16-     {\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/goal-execution-plan.json-17-       \"id\": \"step-02\",\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/goal-execution-plan.json:18:       \"story\": \"Update fragment-usage detection so the unused-fragment rule identifies fragment inclusions from parsed source instead of regex matching.\",\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/goal-execution-plan.json-19-       \"functionalAcceptance\": [\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/goal-execution-plan.json-20-         \"Fragment inclusions present in source files are recognized through the new parser for the purposes of the unused-fragment rule.\",\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/goal-execution-plan.json-18-       \"story\": \"Update fragment-usage detection so the unused-fragment rule identifies fragment inclusions from parsed source instead of regex matching.\",\n.pi/goal/goal-run-mpnrlu69-tbrad3/goal-