ad_to_string(tmp.path().join(\"skillet.lock\")).unwrap();\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-271-     assert!(\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-271-     assert!(\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-272-         lock.contains(\"note\"),\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs:273:         \"lockfile should reference the 'note' fragment\"\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-274-     );\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-275-     assert!(\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-275-     assert!(\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-276-         lock.contains(\"sha256:\"),\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs:277:         \"lockfile should contain a sha256 hash for the fragment\"\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-278-     );\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-279-     assert!(\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-279-     assert!(\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-280-         lock.contains(\"my-skill\"),\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs:281:         \"lockfile fragments section should list 'my-skill' in used_by\"\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-282-     );\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-283-     assert!(\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-283-     assert!(\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-284-         lock.contains(\"tokens\"),\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs:285:         \"lockfile fragment entry should contain a tokens field\"\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-286-     );\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/build_tests.rs-287- }\ndocs/adr/0002-three-layer-ref-detection.md-41- - Precision improves as authors adopt explicit annotations (layer 2)\ndocs/adr/0002-three-layer-ref-detection.md-42- - False positives are minimized (conservative heuristics + suppressions in skillet.toml)\ndocs/adr/0002-three-layer-ref-detection.md:43: - `var::` and `env::` enable DRY without fragments (single-value substitution)\ndocs/adr/0002-three-layer-ref-detection.md-44- \ndocs/adr/0001-preprocessor-architecture.md-5- \ndocs/adr/0001-preprocessor-architecture.md-6- ## Context\ndocs/adr/0001-preprocessor-architecture.md:7: Skillet is a development kit for agent skills — part templating language, part linter, part quality toolkit. The Agent Skills spec defines SKILL.md as plain markdown with YAML frontmatter. We need to support fragments (shared reusable blocks), typed refs, and variable substitution without breaking the spec or requiring agent runtimes to understand skillet-specific syntax.\ndocs/adr/0001-preprocessor-architecture.md-8- \ndocs/adr/0001-preprocessor-architecture.md-9- ## Decision\ndocs/adr/0001-preprocessor-architecture.md-8- \ndocs/adr/0001-preprocessor-architecture.md-9- ## Decision\ndocs/adr/0001-preprocessor-architecture.md:10: Skillet uses a **preprocessor model**: authors write `.skill` source files containing template directives (`{{> fragment }}`, `ref::`, `var::`, `env::`). `skillet build` compiles these to spec-compliant `SKILL.md` files. Compiled output is committed to version control.\ndocs/adr/0001-preprocessor-architecture.md-11- \ndocs/adr/0001-preprocessor-architecture.md-12- ### Key properties:\ndocs/adr/0001-preprocessor-architecture.md-19- 1. **Agent-runtime includes** — define an include format runtimes resolve natively. Rejected: breaks portability, requires runtime adoption.\ndocs/adr/0001-preprocessor-architecture.md-20- 2. **Source-only repo** — don't commit compiled output, consumers must install skillet. Rejected: breaks zero-dependency consumption of skills.\ndocs/adr/0001-preprocessor-architecture.md:21: 3. **In-markdown annotations only** — no build step, just lint existing SKILL.md files. Rejected: can't solve duplication (fragments require compilation).\ndocs/adr/0001-preprocessor-architecture.md-22- \ndocs/adr/0001-preprocessor-architecture.md-23- ## Consequences\ndocs/diy-memory.md-115- |---|---|---|\ndocs/diy-memory.md-116- | Token shape | `TokenKind` enum + `Range<u32>` | Zero allocation; slices back into source via range when text is needed. |\ndocs/diy-memory.md:117: | Fragment delimiters | `{>` open, `<}` close | Simplified from the original `{{> }}` syntax; easier to lex unambiguously. |\ndocs/diy-memory.md-118- | Ref syntax | Backtick-prefixed only: `` `ref::foo` `` | Naked `ref::foo` in prose is BodyText; only backtick-wrapped refs are structural. |\ndocs/diy-memory.md-119- | Tick-context detection | Check `src[start_pos - 1] == b'\\`'` in `tick_prefixed_ref_token` | No state needed; the previous byte tells us if we're inside a backtick context. |\ndocs/diy-memory.md-120- | Double-tick escape | `` ``...`` `` suppresses structural token detection | TickDouble pushed silently, then `make_body` called directly — escaped content never reaches the keyword-detection arm. |\ndocs/diy-memory.md-121- | Code fence | Triple backtick absorbed as single `BodyText` | Delimiters included in range; `make_body` detects and skips internal triple-backtick sequences. |\ndocs/diy-memory.md:122: | Multi-token constructs | `::` and `FragmentOpen`/`FragmentClose` pushed directly inside match arms | Allows one return value per loop iteration while still emitting multiple tokens for `::`-prefixed refs and fragments. |\ndocs/diy-memory.md-123- | Terminator handling | `pos -= 1` after consuming terminator in helpers | Leaves the terminating byte for the outer loop; no data loss. |\ndocs/diy-memory.md-124- | `Vec<Token>` over streaming iterator | Materialise everything | Parser needs arbitrary lookahead; `Vec` is cheap at skillet's scale and dramatically simplifies the parser. |\ndocs/diy-memory.md-146- - **Two-token emission in one iteration** — `DoubleColon` and `FragmentOpen`\ndocs/diy-memory.md-147-   are pushed directly to `tokens` inside the match arm, then the arm returns a\ndocs/diy-memory.md:148:   second token (`RefValue` / fragment id) as its expression value. The `println`\ndocs/diy-memory.md-149-   at the bottom of the loop only captures the return-value token, which confused\ndocs/diy-memory.md-150-   debugging until the full `tokens` vec was inspected.\ndocs/diy-memory.md-157- TickDouble    — double backtick (escape sequence delimiter)\ndocs/diy-memory.md-158- DoubleColon   — `::` separator between ref type and ref value\ndocs/diy-memory.md:159: FragmentOpen  — `{>` fragment insertion opener\ndocs/diy-memory.md-160- FragmentClose — `<}` fragment insertion closer\ndocs/diy-memory.md-161- RefValue      — the value after `::` or the name inside a fragment\ndocs/diy-memory.md-158- DoubleColon   — `::` separator between ref type and ref value\ndocs/diy-memory.md-159- FragmentOpen  — `{>` fragment insertion opener\ndocs/diy-memory.md:160: FragmentClose — `<}` fragment insertion closer\ndocs/diy-memory.md-161- RefValue      — the value after `::` or the name inside a fragment\ndocs/diy-memory.md-162- RefReference  — `ref` keyword\ndocs/diy-memory.md-159- FragmentOpen  — `{>` fragment insertion opener\ndocs/diy-memory.md-160- FragmentClose — `<}` fragment insertion closer\ndocs/diy-memory.md:161: RefValue      — the value after `::` or the name inside a fragment\ndocs/diy-memory.md-162- RefReference  — `ref` keyword\ndocs/diy-memory.md-163- RefSKill      — `skill` keyword\ndocs/diy-memory.md-169- ### What was easier than expected\ndocs/diy-memory.md-170- \ndocs/diy-memory.md:171: - The `make_body` / `make_ref_value` / `make_fragment_id` helper pattern clicked\ndocs/diy-memory.md-172-   quickly once the \"unconsume terminator\" rule was established.\ndocs/diy-memory.md-173- - `starts_with` on byte slices made keyword detection trivial.\ndocs/diy-memory.md-256-   its own node so the compiler can issue a lint suggestion.\ndocs/diy-memory.md-257- \ndocs/diy-memory.md:258: - **Syntax mismatch with existing `.pan` files** — example files use `{{> name }}`\ndocs/diy-memory.md-259-   (old syntax); lexer uses `{>` / `<}` (new syntax). Examples are integration\ndocs/diy-memory.md-260-   tests with the full toolchain and must not change until the whole pipeline\ndocs/diy-memory.md-257- \ndocs/diy-memory.md-258- - **Syntax mismatch with existing `.pan` files** — example files use `{{> name }}`\ndocs/diy-memory.md:259:   (old syntax); lexer uses `{>` / `<}` (new syntax). Examples are integration\ndocs/diy-memory.md-260-   tests with the full toolchain and must not change until the whole pipeline\ndocs/diy-memory.md-261-   is swapped over. New-syntax unit tests are the correct test surface for the\ndocs/diy-memory.md-286- \ndocs/diy-memory.md-287- - Plain `match` or `Visitor` trait?\ndocs/diy-memory.md:288: - Where does fragment expansion happen — inline during walk, or a separate pass?\ndocs/diy-memory.md-289- - How does the `CompileContext` get built, and when?\ndocs/diy-memory.md-290- - Token counting: as-you-emit or end-pass?\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/init_tests.rs-9-     fs::create_dir_all(tmp.join(&cfg.workspace.skills_src_dir)).unwrap();\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/init_tests.rs-10-     fs::create_dir_all(tmp.join(&cfg.workspace.skills_out_dir)).unwrap();\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/init_tests.rs:11:     fs::create_dir_all(tmp.join(&cfg.workspace.fragments_dir)).unwrap();\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/init_tests.rs-12- }\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/init_tests.rs-13- \ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/init_tests.rs-13- \ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/init_tests.rs-14- #[test]\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/init_tests.rs:15: fn init_creates_skills_dir_fragments_dir_and_config() {\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/init_tests.rs-16-     // Arrange\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/init_tests.rs-17-     let tmp = TempDir::new().unwrap();\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/init_tests.rs-29-     // Assert — filesystem layout\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/init_tests.rs-30-     assert!(tmp.path().join(\"src/skills\").is_dir());\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/init_tests.rs:31:     assert!(tmp.path().join(\"src/skills/_fragments\").is_dir());\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/init_tests.rs-32-     assert!(tmp.path().join(\"skills\").is_dir());\ncrates/skillet-cli/tests/init_t